            <span id="out-health-rating" class="text-xl font-bold text-amber-400 font-mono mt-1 block">B+ (Balanced)</span>
            <span class="text-xs text-slate-500 font-mono">Runway Sustainable</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <script is:raw>
        function updateSaaSCalc() {
          const sm = parseFloat(document.getElementById('calc-sm-spend').value) || 1;
          const arr = parseFloat(document.getElementById('calc-net-arr').value) || 1;
          const gm = (parseFloat(document.getElementById('calc-gm-pct').value) || 80) / 100.0;

          const unadjMonths = (sm / arr) * 12.0;
          const adjMonths = (sm / (arr * gm)) * 12.0;
          const magicNumber = arr / sm;

          document.getElementById('out-unadj-payback').textContent = unadjMonths.toFixed(1) + ' mos';
          document.getElementById('out-adj-payback').textContent = adjMonths.toFixed(1) + ' mos';
          document.getElementById('out-magic-num').textContent = magicNumber.toFixed(2);

          let rating = 'C (Inefficient)';
          let color = '#f87171';
          if (adjMonths <= 9.0) {
            rating = 'A+ (Top Decile)';
            color = '#34d399';
          } else if (adjMonths <= 15.0) {
            rating = 'A (Efficient)';
            color = '#10b981';
          } else if (adjMonths <= 20.0) {
            rating = 'B+ (Sustainable)';
            color = '#fbbf24';
          } else if (adjMonths <= 26.0) {
            rating = 'B- (Stretched)';
            color = '#f59e0b';
          }

          const elem = document.getElementById('out-health-rating');
          elem.textContent = rating;
          elem.style.color = color;
        }

        document.getElementById('calc-sm-spend').addEventListener('input', updateSaaSCalc);
        document.getElementById('calc-net-arr').addEventListener('input', updateSaaSCalc);
        document.getElementById('calc-gm-pct').addEventListener('input', updateSaaSCalc);
        updateSaaSCalc();
      </script>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        The SaaS Magic Number vs. CAC Payback: Inverse Efficiency Mathematics
      </h2>
      <p>
        The SaaS Magic Number and CAC Payback are mathematically dual representations of go-to-market productivity. While CAC Payback evaluates time-to-breakeven on an individual logo or cohort, the Magic Number measures aggregate revenue expansion per dollar of commercial investment:
      </p>

      <pre is:raw class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 text-xs font-mono text-emerald-300 overflow-x-auto"><code>SaaS Magic Number = [ (Quarterly GAAP Revenue_t - Quarterly GAAP Revenue_t-1) * 4 ] / S&M Spend_t-1
Unadjusted Payback Months ~= 12 / Magic Number
</code></pre>

      <ul class="space-y-3 text-slate-300 list-disc pl-5">
        <li><strong>Magic Number &gt; 1.20 (Payback &lt; 10 Months)</strong>: Highly efficient growth flywheel. The business should accelerate capital deployment into paid marketing channels, sales headcount, and pipeline expansion.</li>
        <li><strong>Magic Number 0.75 – 1.00 (Payback 12 to 16 Months)</strong>: Standard institutional benchmark for venture-backed B2B SaaS. GTM operations are healthy; growth should be calibrated against cash runway.</li>
        <li><strong>Magic Number &lt; 0.50 (Payback &gt; 24 Months)</strong>: Severe capital leakage. Expanding the sales force under this profile accelerates enterprise cash burn without yielding proportionate enterprise value. Leadership must halt quota expansion and audit pipeline disqualification rates.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        The Blended Payback Trap: Channel &amp; Segment Disaggregation
      </h2>
      <p>
        The single most common mistake made by growth-stage finance teams is reporting an aggregate <em>blended CAC payback</em> across the entire business. Blended metrics conceal toxic operational realities:
      </p>
      <ol class="space-y-3 text-slate-300 list-decimal pl-5">
        <li>
          <strong>Cross-Subsidization Blindspots</strong>: A blended 14-month payback frequently hides a phenomenal self-serve Product-Led Growth (PLG) engine recovering acquisition spend in 5 months, which is quietly masking an enterprise outbound field team operating at a disastrous 32-month payback.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Channel Attribution Divergence</strong>: Organic technical SEO, documentation traffic, and developer word-of-mouth typically boast CAC paybacks under 4 months. In contrast, paid Google Search keywords (targeting hyper-competitive intent like <em>best CRM software</em>) frequently exhibit paybacks exceeding 28 months once SDR qualification overhead is factored in.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Sales Cycle Time-Lag Distortion</strong>: Because an enterprise sales deal may require 6 to 9 months from initial outbound contact to contract execution, matching current quarter marketing expenses against current quarter closed ARR creates massive statistical distortion. CFOs must enforce a trailing sales-cycle lag (e.g. comparing Q3 closed revenue against Q1/Q2 marketing outlays) to reflect true cash-recovery velocity.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Multi-Touch Attribution Discrepancies</strong>: Assigning 100% of acquisition credit to first-touch ads or last-touch demos penalizes nurturing touchpoints. Deploying W-shaped or U-shaped position-based attribution allocates 40% to first discovery, 40% to opportunity conversion, and 20% across intermediate technical webinars, yielding accurate per-channel payback precision.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
